Experiment 7: CPU-Bound vs I/O-Bound C Programs Using top
A comprehensive technical manual analyzing CPU scheduling dynamics, comparing CPU-bound and I/O-bound process behaviors in C, background execution via job control, and process state monitoring using top.
Experiment 7: CPU-Bound and I/O-Bound C Programs Using top
1. Aim
To write and run a CPU-bound C program and an I/O-bound C program, and to compare their CPU utilization, process states and accumulated CPU time using the top command.
2. Program 1: I/O-Bound Program
Filename: io_bound.c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int main()
{
    FILE *fp;

    printf("Starting I/O task...\n");

    while (1)
    {
        fp = fopen("dummy.txt", "w");

        if (fp != NULL)
        {
            fprintf(fp, "Writing disk data\n");
            fclose(fp);

            printf("File operation done\n");
        }

        sleep(1);
    }

    return 0;
}


2.1 Line-by-Line Explanation
Code
Explanation
#include <stdio.h>
Provides standard input/output and file-handling functions such as printf(), fopen(), fprintf() and fclose().
#include <unistd.h>
Provides the sleep() function on Linux and other POSIX systems.
int main()
Program execution begins from the main() function.
FILE *fp;
Declares a file pointer named fp that holds the file stream returned by fopen().
printf("Starting I/O task...\n");
Displays a message indicating that the I/O task has started.
while (1)
Creates an infinite loop. The file operation repeats until the process is terminated.
fp = fopen("dummy.txt", "w");
Opens dummy.txt in write mode. If the file does not exist, it is created. If it exists, its previous contents are truncated. If the operation fails, fopen() returns NULL.
if (fp != NULL)
Checks that the file was opened successfully before writing to it.
fprintf(fp, "Writing disk data\n");
Writes the text to the file stream. The data may initially be buffered in memory.
fclose(fp);
Closes the file and flushes the C library's buffered output. This does not guarantee that the data has been physically written to the storage device when the function returns.
printf("File operation done\n");
Displays a confirmation message after the file has been closed.
sleep(1);
Suspends the process for approximately one second. During this time the process does not actively consume CPU time.
return 0;
Indicates successful termination. It is normally unreachable because the loop runs indefinitely.


2.2 Expected Output
Starting I/O task...
File operation done
File operation done
File operation done
...


After the initial startup message, the confirmation message appears approximately once per second. Actual timing can vary.
3. Program 2: CPU-Bound Program
Filename: cpu_bound.c
#include <stdio.h>

int main()
{
    long long counter = 0;

    while (1)
    {
        counter++;

        if (counter % 100000000 == 0)
        {
            printf("CPU task running: %lld\n", counter);
        }
    }

    return 0;
}


3.1 Line-by-Line Explanation
Code
Explanation
#include <stdio.h>
Includes the standard input/output library, which provides printf().
int main()
Program execution starts here.
long long counter = 0;
Declares a variable named counter and initializes it to zero. The long long type can hold a very large integer.
while (1)
Starts an infinite loop that continues until the process is terminated.
counter++;
Increments the counter by one on every iteration (1, 2, 3, and so on).
if (counter % 100000000 == 0)
The % operator gives the remainder. The condition is true whenever the counter is exactly divisible by 100,000,000 (for example 100000000, 200000000, 300000000).
printf("CPU task running: %lld\n", counter);
Prints the current counter value. The %lld format specifier is used for a long long integer.
return 0;
Indicates successful termination, but the infinite loop normally prevents this statement from being reached.


3.2 Expected Output
CPU task running: 100000000
CPU task running: 200000000
CPU task running: 300000000
...


Output appears each time the counter reaches another multiple of 100 million. Printing uses a small amount of CPU time, but most of the work is repeated counting.
4. Compiling and Running the Programs
Step 1: Compile
gcc io_bound.c -o io_bound
gcc cpu_bound.c -o cpu_bound


Step 2: Run both programs in the background
./io_bound &
./cpu_bound &


The & symbol runs each program in the background.
Step 3: Find the PIDs
pgrep -af 'io_bound|cpu_bound'


Example output:
2451 ./io_bound
2452 ./cpu_bound


Use your actual PIDs in the commands that follow.
5. Observing CPU Share Using top
Purpose
Command
Notes
Display all processes
top
Press P to sort processes by CPU utilization.
Monitor only these two processes
top -p 2451,2452
Replace the example PIDs with the actual process IDs.
Refresh every second
top -d 1
Updates the display once per second.
Collect five snapshots
top -b -n 5 -d 1
Useful for recording readings in the observation table.
View individual CPU cores
top, then press 1
Shows utilization of each logical CPU.


Expected observations
cpu_bound should generally show a high %CPU.
io_bound should generally show a low %CPU, because it sleeps for one second after each file operation.
The CPU-bound process generally accumulates CPU time faster.
Note: A single-threaded process may use approximately 100% of one logical CPU, depending on system load and top display settings. The precise value can vary.


